-- tartaric acid, where two substances share one bond graph and the difference between them is one bit.
-- Atoms: 0 the first carboxyl carbon with oxygens 1 and 2, 3 the first stereocentre with hydrogen 4 and
-- hydroxyl oxygen 5 carrying hydrogen 6; then the other half, 7 to 13 in the same order. The one operation
-- either substance has is the exchange of the two halves.
--
-- In the (R,R) acid the halves are related by a two-fold rotation, which is proper: the two methine
-- hydrogens are homotopic. In the meso acid they are related by a mirror plane, which is improper: the two
-- methine hydrogens are enantiotopic. The bond graph is the same graph in both cases, the permutation is
-- the same permutation, and the only difference in the record is whether that permutation is proper.
--
-- This is Pasteur's compound. In 1848 he separated the two chiral crystal forms of sodium ammonium
-- tartrate by hand under a lens, which is the first separation of enantiomers ever made, and the chiral
-- agent that made it possible was his own handedness in sorting them.
tar-halves : Nat → Nat
tar-halves = nat-xch (cons 0 (cons 1 (cons 2 (cons 3 (cons 4 (cons 5 (cons 6 nil))))))) (cons 7 (cons 8 (cons 9 (cons 10 (cons 11 (cons 12 (cons 13 nil)))))))
